WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.079
Let's talk about the agentic loop, the core cycle that drives every agent. In this video,

2
00:00:06.079 --> 00:00:12.319
we'll walk through all four phases, perception, reasoning, action, and observation. We'll see

3
00:00:12.319 --> 00:00:17.840
how they connect to one another, and we'll look at an example of how this works to make it concrete.

4
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.479
By the end, you'll have a clear mental model of how agents actually work.

5
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.319
An agent isn't one call and one answer. It is a cycle that keeps turning

6
00:00:28.399 --> 00:00:34.319
until the work is done, and that cycle has a name. The agentic loop is the repeating, perceive,

7
00:00:34.319 --> 00:00:41.520
plan, act, observe pattern. The model reads its context, picks an action, runs it, gets a result,

8
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:48.319
then decides whether to keep going or stop. Tool use makes the action half real. The model emits

9
00:00:48.319 --> 00:00:53.599
a structured call, the runtime executes it, and the result comes back as an observation.

10
00:00:54.319 --> 00:01:00.400
Dynamic planning is what makes the loop powerful. Because the model isn't following a fixed script,

11
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.080
it updates its plan every turn based on what it just learned. Put all of that together with

12
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:12.879
a runtime that executes the calls, and you have an agentic system. Perception is the first phase,

13
00:01:12.879 --> 00:01:18.000
and it's deceptively simple. The model reads everything currently in the context window.

14
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.279
That includes the original instructions, the conversation history, any documents loaded at

15
00:01:23.279 --> 00:01:30.160
startup, and critically, every tool result that's accumulated so far. The model uses all of that to

16
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.599
figure out where it is in the task, what's been done, what information is available, what's still

17
00:01:35.599 --> 00:01:41.440
unknown. Think of perception as the agent taking stock before it decides its next move. If the

18
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:47.279
context window is the agent's working memory, then perception is the act of reading from that memory.

19
00:01:47.279 --> 00:01:52.639
It's not passive. The model is actively identifying relevant signals against the

20
00:01:52.639 --> 00:01:58.480
background of everything else in context. Reasoning follows perception. Once the model

21
00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:04.000
knows what's available, it decides what to do next. This is where dynamic planning shows up.

22
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.559
The agent isn't running a fixed workflow where step three always follows step two.

23
00:02:08.559 --> 00:02:12.800
It's weighing what it just perceived against the original goal and asking,

24
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:18.240
what action would move me closer to a solution? Sometimes the answer is obvious. Sometimes the

25
00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.199
agent has to work through ambiguity. The model might recognize that a prior search returned

26
00:02:23.199 --> 00:02:28.800
incomplete data and decide to refine the query before proceeding. The key point is that reasoning

27
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:34.639
is adaptive. If the situation has changed since the last iteration, the plan can change too.

28
00:02:34.639 --> 00:02:39.360
That flexibility is exactly what makes agentic systems more capable than simple pipelines.

29
00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.839
Action and observation are two sides of the same coin, so let's look at them together. In the action

30
00:02:45.839 --> 00:02:52.240
phase, the model emits a structured tool call. It might call a search API, run a code interpreter,

31
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:58.800
query a database, or write a final response. The tool call is the moment the agent reaches out to

32
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:05.759
the external world. Observation is what comes back. The runtime executes the tool, gets a result,

33
00:03:05.919 --> 00:03:12.160
and injects that result into the context window. Now the agent has new information it didn't have

34
00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:17.839
before. That observation is what the next perception phase will read. These two phases

35
00:03:17.839 --> 00:03:23.440
are tightly linked. Every action should produce a meaningful observation, and every observation

36
00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:29.279
should inform the next decision. The context window is the agent's working memory,

37
00:03:29.279 --> 00:03:34.479
and it is worth understanding how it fills up over a session. At the start, it holds inputs,

38
00:03:34.479 --> 00:03:41.679
the system prompt, the user's request, any initial documents. As the loop runs, actions accumulate,

39
00:03:41.679 --> 00:03:48.080
tool calls pile up, observations accumulate too. Every tool result, meaning the structured data,

40
00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:55.039
the runtime hands back after a call executes, gets appended. Intermediate reasoning adds to the total

41
00:03:55.039 --> 00:04:00.800
as well. All of that is in context memory. It is immediately accessible to the model,

42
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:07.199
and it is completely ephemeral. When the session ends, or the context gets truncated, it is gone.

43
00:04:07.199 --> 00:04:12.479
That is why a long-running agent can fill its window, if it makes many calls without summarizing

44
00:04:12.479 --> 00:04:18.559
or pruning, and why you design for that constraint from the start. The cyclic nature of the loop is

45
00:04:18.559 --> 00:04:24.959
what makes agents capable of complex multi-step tasks. Let's trace the data flow. Perception

46
00:04:24.959 --> 00:04:30.239
surfaces what's in context for the reasoning phase. Reasoning produces an action selection

47
00:04:30.239 --> 00:04:35.359
based on that view. The action generates an observation that gets returned to context,

48
00:04:35.359 --> 00:04:42.079
and then perception reads that updated context at the start of the next iteration. Each phase's output

49
00:04:42.079 --> 00:04:47.600
is the next phase's input. This isn't a linear pipeline, it's a feedback loop. That feedback is

50
00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:54.000
what allows an agent to course correct, to retry when a tool fails, to revise its approach when the

51
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.399
data it expected turns out to be different from what it actually received. Let's make the loop

52
00:05:00.399 --> 00:05:06.640
concrete with a research agent example. The user asks the agent to summarize recent findings on a

53
00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:12.480
topic. Perception. The agent reads the query and notices it has no prior search results

54
00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:19.279
in context yet. Reasoning. It decides to run a search. Action. It calls the search API tool

55
00:05:19.279 --> 00:05:25.679
with a targeted query. Observation. The runtime returns a list of article summaries and injects

56
00:05:25.679 --> 00:05:32.720
them into context. Now the loop restarts. Perception. The agent reads those results

57
00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:39.279
and notices one source looks highly relevant. Reasoning. It decides to fetch the full text

58
00:05:39.279 --> 00:05:45.040
of that article, and on it goes. Each iteration makes progress toward the goal, and the agent's

59
00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:51.839
plan evolves based on what it finds. When an agent feels dumb, it's usually not the reasoning

60
00:05:51.839 --> 00:05:57.440
that broke. It's that the result of the last action never made it back into the context the

61
00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:03.760
model reads next. The loop only gets smarter because each pass can see what the previous one

62
00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:09.440
already tried. Break that feedback loop, and you've got a very expensive random guesser.

63
00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:13.040
That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

